      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nPython's `SamplingParams.from_optional` clamps every `logit_bias` value to `[-100, 100]` for every entry point, so a bias of `250` behaves like `100`. The Rust frontend forwards the value to the engine unchanged, so the same request samples differently on the two frontends. This PR clamps biases in `vllm_text::lower_sampling_params`.\n\nSupersedes the inactive draft vllm-project/vllm#50350, which clamps in the HTTP-only `convert_logit_bias` helper.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Rust clamps each `logit_bias` value to `[-100, 100]` before it reaches engine-core, matching `SamplingParams.from_optional`.\n- The clamp applies to every Rust entry point: `/v1/chat/completions`, `/v1/completions`, `/inference/v1/generate`, and gRPC.\n- In-range values are unchanged; key parsing and the out-of-vocab check are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\nCommands from `.buildkite/scripts/run-rust-frontend-cargo-ci.sh` (`style-clippy` and `test` modes), run from the repo root:\n\n```\ncargo fmt --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --all -- --check   # ok\ncargo sort --workspace --check rust                          # ok\ncargo deny --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --config rust/deny.toml check bans   # ok\ncargo clippy --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings   # ok\ncargo nextest run --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --workspace --all-features --locked --no-fail-fast\n# 2140 tests run: 2140 passed, 13 skipped\n```\n\nNew test: `vllm-text` `lower_sampling_params_clamps_logit_bias` (`{5: 250, 6: -300, 7: 1.5}` lowers to `{5: 100, 6: -100, 7: 1.5}`). With the fix reverted and the test kept, it fails: `left: Some({6: -300.0, 5: 250.0, 7: 1.5})`.\n\n## Details\n\n- **Where the clamp lives.** `vllm_text::lower_sampling_params` turns northbound sampling params into engine-core params, and every Rust entry point goes through it. Python clamps at the equivalent point, `SamplingParams` construction. Clamping in `convert_logit_bias` (as in vllm-project/vllm#50350) would miss `/inference/v1/generate` and gRPC, which do not use that helper.\n- **NaN.** `f32::clamp` keeps NaN, while Python's `min(100.0, max(-100.0, nan))` returns `-100.0`. Over HTTP this cannot happen, since `serde_json` rejects a body with a `NaN` literal; over gRPC a NaN float would pass through unclamped. This PR leaves NaN alone: the open draft vllm-project/vllm#62038 proposes rejecting NaN biases in Python instead, and Rust should follow whichever behavior lands there.\n- **Not a duplicate.** Searched open PRs for `logit_bias` (`clamp`, `Rust`, title-only). vllm-project/vllm#50350 is the only open PR for this item: a draft with no activity since 2026-09-24 that clamps in the HTTP-only helper. vllm-project/vllm#62038 (Python NaN rejection) and vllm-project/vllm#60562 (V2 sampler caps) touch different behavior.\n- AI assistance (Claude Code) was used to write this change; the human submitter reviewed every changed line and the validation results.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
